{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-3217","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"3217","title":"Stop Child Hunger Act of 2025","introducedDate":"2025-05-06","latestActionDate":"2025-05-06","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on Education and Workforce.","policyArea":"Agriculture and Food","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000593","cosponsorBioguideIds":["H001081","M001160","S001216"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Stop Child Hunger Act of 2025 This bill expands the Department of Agriculture's (USDA's) Summer Electronic Benefit Transfer Program for Children (Summer EBT) to include coverage during a school closure period. (Summer EBT provides grocery-buying benefits to low-income families with school-aged children when schools are closed for the summer.) Under the bill, a school closure period means a period in which an elementary school or secondary school is closed, operating remotely, or operating in a hybrid manner for five or more consecutive weekdays during a calendar year. The bill also increases the daily value of the benefit to cover the cost of breakfast, lunch, and a snack for every day school is closed. For FY2026, USDA must pay each state agency and covered tribal organization 100% of the administrative expenses (currently 50%) incurred in operating the program. The rate that USDA must pay for administrative expenses decreases each fiscal year until it reaches 50% of the expenses for FY2031 and each fiscal year thereafter. USDA must also provide grants to states to support the development or upgrading of data systems that are necessary to implement Summer EBT.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/3217","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr3217.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:48.302Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-3217","plainSummary":"This bill expands a federal program that provides grocery benefits to low-income families with school-aged children during summer breaks. It would extend the program to cover any period when schools are closed, remote, or hybrid for five or more consecutive weekdays, increase the daily benefit amount to cover breakfast, lunch, and a snack, and temporarily raise federal reimbursement of state administrative costs from 50% to 100% in 2026, phasing back down to 50% by 2031. The bill also authorizes grants to help states build or upgrade data systems needed to run the program.","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"State agencies and tribal organizations would receive increased federal funding for administrative expenses and data system development grants."},{"industry":"agriculture","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill increases federal spending on grocery benefits redeemable at food retailers, directly affecting food distribution and sales volumes."},{"industry":"retail-consumer","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Grocery retailers and food sellers would see increased transaction volume from expanded electronic benefit transfers for food purchases."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"6a0fde014ac822d93575d90f","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:47:34.072Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would widen the USDA's Summer Electronic Benefit Transfer Program for Children (Summer EBT) to include coverage during a school closure period.","whoItTouches":"Farmers, and anyone who buys food.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in farming, and food prices. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hr-3217","bioguideId":"H001081","cycle":2026,"score":0.015192784508462282,"matches":[{"industry":"agriculture","dono